{
  "id": "20-phrasing-strokes",
  "title": "Phrasing in practice",
  "goal": "Worked examples, what it saves, and what the grammar leaves out.",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "t": "h",
      "x": "Putting the parts together"
    },
    {
      "t": "table",
      "head": [
        "Phrase",
        "Parts"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "[[it was|KPWHEB]]",
          "it + past + be"
        ],
        [
          "[[it is|KPWH-B]]",
          "it + be"
        ],
        [
          "[[I am|SWR-B]]",
          "I + be"
        ],
        [
          "[[I am not|SWR*B]]",
          "I + not + be"
        ],
        [
          "[[I know|SWR-PB]]",
          "I + know"
        ],
        [
          "[[I don't know|SWR*PB]]",
          "I + not + know"
        ],
        [
          "[[I didn't know|SWR*EPB]]",
          "I + past + not + know"
        ],
        [
          "[[it would be|KPWHOEB]]",
          "it + would + be"
        ],
        [
          "[[he had been|STWHEFB]]",
          "he + past + have been"
        ],
        [
          "[[I couldn't have been|SWRA*EFB]]",
          "I + could + not + have been"
        ],
        [
          "[[they weren't|STWHR*EB]]",
          "they + past + not + be"
        ],
        [
          "[[you see|KPWR-SZ]]",
          "you + see"
        ],
        [
          "[[I do|SWR]]",
          "I alone"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "p",
      "x": "Steno order: left bank (starter), vowels and star, right bank. With no vowels or star, `-` marks the start of the right bank, as in {{I am}} [[SWR-B]]."
    },
    {
      "t": "h",
      "x": "There"
    },
    {
      "t": "p",
      "x": "*there* takes *be*, *have been* and the modals only, not the other verbs, and no bare *do*: [[there is|STHR-B]], [[there was|STHREB]], [[there would be|STHROEB]], [[there must have been|STHRAUFB]]."
    },
    {
      "t": "h",
      "x": "Where it sits in the dictionary"
    },
    {
      "t": "p",
      "x": "The generator fixes the grammar's 3,758 strokes before any word settles, as it does the suffix strokes, so every one is placed and a word whose own candidates landed on one took its next outline: 122 words changed an outline, and of the 5,000 commonest only {{pity}} and {{impress}} moved their primary. The merged two-word phrases leave the texts the grammar writes to it (*it is* is [[it is|KPWH-B]]). Every word keeps an outline."
    },
    {
      "t": "h",
      "x": "What it saves"
    },
    {
      "t": "table",
      "head": [
        "Text",
        "Words",
        "Strokes without the grammar",
        "With the grammar"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Five Gutenberg novels",
          "712,328",
          "618,285",
          "608,651 (1.6% fewer)"
        ],
        [
          "Quote passages",
          "3,105",
          "2,976",
          "2,960 (0.5% fewer)"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "p",
      "x": "Measured by `python3 tools/phrasing_prototype.py --savings BEFORE` against a build made without the grammar, the merged two-word phrases counted in both. The novels are third-person past narration; everyday first- and second-person present typing has more of these pairs per word, so this undercounts: the extracted quote passages of the test corpus, which are speech, spend 17% fewer strokes with phrases and grammar than word by word."
    },
    {
      "t": "h",
      "x": "Looking one up"
    },
    {
      "t": "pre",
      "x": "lyk lookup \"they wouldn't\"\nlyk lookup \"I do not know\"\nlyk lookup SWRAB"
    },
    {
      "t": "p",
      "x": "The lookup puts the grammar's stroke first, with the key group that says each part (`STWHR starter (they) + OE auxiliary (would) + * not`), then each word on its own. Given a stroke it says which phrase it is. The site's Lookup does the same; details: [looking a phrase up](#/ref/phrases/looking-a-phrase-up)."
    },
    {
      "t": "h",
      "x": "Not covered yet"
    },
    {
      "t": "list",
      "items": [
        "Questions: *do you*, *are you*, *can I* (the starter after the auxiliary).",
        "*that*, *this*, *what*, *who* as starters.",
        "*I'm* and *it's*: contracted positives.",
        "More verbs, chosen by measured gain."
      ]
    },
    {
      "t": "h",
      "x": "The commonest phrases"
    },
    {
      "t": "p",
      "x": "The forty commonest in running text (Gutenberg corpus count), commonest first; the drill below is exactly this list."
    }
